# Bulk data — NEVER commit. `data/` holds only regenerable artefacts: the ~135 MB ITF
# snapshot (re-fetchable), its Parquet derivatives, cached MPEC HTML, and Find_Orb working
# directories. Ignored wholesale so a new working directory can never be added by accident.
#
# One exception in spirit: `data/snapshots/` is the ITF archive, and history only
# accumulates forward, so it genuinely cannot be regenerated. It is still far too large for
# git and needs its own backup if it is ever to be relied on.
# Anchored with a leading slash: an unanchored `data/` would also swallow `tests/data/`,
# which holds the committed fixtures the whole test suite runs against.
/data/
*.parquet
*.txt.gz

# Generated reports. The committed narrative lives in the M*-RESULTS.md files; the JSON
# behind them is regenerable and runs to hundreds of megabytes -- M4's m4-new.json alone
# is 108 MB, above GitHub's hard 100 MB limit.
#
# Matched by PATTERN, not by an enumerated list. The list version silently failed to cover
# M4 because its reports are named m4-new.json / m4-old.json rather than m4-report.json,
# and the oversized files reached a push before anything caught them. A per-milestone
# filename list is a rule that has to be remembered; a pattern is one that does not.
/m[0-9]*.json

# Submission payloads and watcher runtime state are private generated artifacts. The
# public workflow never creates a payload; local review may. Keep both out of git even
# when someone uses `git add .` from this directory.
/out/submission-*.json
/out/submission-watch-*.md

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Secrets (no credentials are needed today; belt-and-suspenders for M3 submission work)
.env
*.token
secrets/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
